IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`
IMPORT `oapa-seat`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- LAYER 2 CASES: worked cases for every question of oapa-seat.l4.
--
-- Each case is a set of facts a person in the seat could state, with the answer decided from the text of the section
-- (never from the code). Booleans are written TRUE / FALSE. Every question that takes inputs has at least one case that
-- satisfies it and one that does not; the four questions that take none are asserted once each (section 44's time limit,
-- the two "does this text tell me" questions, and the Scotland question). Every threshold has a case on each side
-- (section 27: 23 and 24 months; section 42: 200 and 201 pounds, 6 and 7 months; section 57: 6 and 7 years; the custodial
-- comparisons at the limit and one above it).
-- The first case of each offence question that satisfies it also checks the three statements the Act makes about the
-- offence (what it calls it, how it is tried, the most that can follow) in the words the seat uses for them.
--
-- CASES OUTSIDE THE ENCODING: whether what happened is a murder or a manslaughter. The deposited text is silent there
-- (sections 1 and 2 are rows of dots and section 5 states only the penalty), so each question returns the explicit value
-- `the deposited text does not say`, asserted below, and never a guess. The Scotland question (section 78) is a different
-- kind: it returns the Act's own answer, which is that the Act does not extend there.

§ `Cases for the seat`

§§ `Murder, manslaughter and killing abroad`

-- inputs, in order: 1 I solicited someone to murder another person | 2 I encouraged someone to murder another person | 3 I persuaded someone to murder another person | 4 I tried to persuade someone to murder another person | 5 I proposed to someone that they murder another person | 6 what I put to them would be a murder
`s4 solicited` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `s4 solicited`'s `the facts make out the offence`
#ASSERT `s4 solicited`'s `what the Act calls it` EQUALS "a misdemeanor"
#ASSERT `s4 solicited`'s `how the Act says it is tried` EQUALS "the text does not say"
#ASSERT `s4 solicited`'s `the most the Act states can follow` EQUALS "imprisonment for life"
`s4 encouraged` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `s4 encouraged`'s `the facts make out the offence`
`s4 persuaded` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `s4 persuaded`'s `the facts make out the offence`
`s4 tried to persuade` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `s4 tried to persuade`'s `the facts make out the offence`
`s4 proposed` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `s4 proposed`'s `the facts make out the offence`
`s4 did none of the five` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`s4 did none of the five`'s `the facts make out the offence`)
#ASSERT `s4 did none of the five`'s `what the Act calls it` EQUALS "Not made out on the facts you gave."
#ASSERT `s4 did none of the five`'s `how the Act says it is tried` EQUALS "Not applicable."
#ASSERT `s4 did none of the five`'s `the most the Act states can follow` EQUALS "Nothing follows from this section on the facts you gave."
`s4 proposed something that would not be a murder` MEANS `Could what I said amount to soliciting or proposing a murder` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s4 proposed something that would not be a murder`'s `the facts make out the offence`)

-- Outside the encoding: the deposited text does not say what a murder or a manslaughter is.
#ASSERT `Does this text tell me whether what I did is a murder` EQUALS `the deposited text does not say`
#ASSERT `Does this text tell me whether what I did is a manslaughter` EQUALS `the deposited text does not say`
-- s 5: the penalty, once convicted.
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of manslaughter` TRUE EQUALS "penal servitude for life"
#ASSERT `What is the most that can follow if I am convicted of manslaughter` FALSE EQUALS "Nothing follows under section 5 until you have been convicted of manslaughter."

-- inputs, in order: 1 what happened is a murder or a manslaughter | 2 it happened on land outside the United Kingdom | 3 I am a subject of Her Majesty
-- s9: a subject kills on land abroad: tried at home
#ASSERT `Can I be tried in England or Ireland for a killing abroad` TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s9: not a murder or manslaughter
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland for a killing abroad` FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- s9: killed at sea, not on land
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland for a killing abroad` TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- s9: not a subject of Her Majesty
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland for a killing abroad` TRUE TRUE FALSE)

-- inputs, in order: 1 what happened is a murder or a manslaughter | 2 the killing was outside England and Ireland
-- s9 proviso: a killing outside England and Ireland: a trial there is not prevented
#ASSERT `Does the Act leave it open to try me outside England and Ireland for a killing abroad` TRUE TRUE
-- s9 proviso: a killing in England: the proviso is silent
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act leave it open to try me outside England and Ireland for a killing abroad` TRUE FALSE)
-- s9 proviso: not a murder or manslaughter
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act leave it open to try me outside England and Ireland for a killing abroad` FALSE TRUE)

-- inputs, in order: 1 I criminally hurt a person | 2 where the person was hurt | 3 the person died of it | 4 where the person died | 5 it amounts to murder or manslaughter
-- s10: hurt at sea, died in England
#ASSERT `Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `upon the sea` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE
-- s10: hurt abroad on land, died in Ireland
#ASSERT `Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `at a place out of England or Ireland, other than the sea` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE
-- s10: hurt in England, died at sea
#ASSERT `Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE `upon the sea` TRUE
-- s10: hurt in England, died abroad on land
#ASSERT `Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE `at a place out of England or Ireland, other than the sea` TRUE
-- s10: hurt and died in England
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE)
-- s10: hurt at sea, died abroad on land
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `upon the sea` TRUE `at a place out of England or Ireland, other than the sea` TRUE)
-- s10: hurt at sea, did not die
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `upon the sea` FALSE `in England or Ireland` TRUE)
-- s10: not criminally hurt
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` FALSE `upon the sea` TRUE `in England or Ireland` TRUE)
-- s10: not murder or manslaughter
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I be tried in England or Ireland where the hurt and the death happened in different places` TRUE `upon the sea` TRUE `in England or Ireland` FALSE)

§§ `Threats to kill`

-- inputs, in order: 1 I made a threat to someone | 2 I had a lawful excuse for making it | 3 I meant them to fear it would be carried out | 4 the threat was to kill them | 5 the threat was to kill someone else
`s16 threat to kill them` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `s16 threat to kill them`'s `the facts make out the offence`
#ASSERT `s16 threat to kill them`'s `what the Act calls it` EQUALS "an offence (the section uses no other word)"
#ASSERT `s16 threat to kill them`'s `how the Act says it is tried` EQUALS "on indictment"
#ASSERT `s16 threat to kill them`'s `the most the Act states can follow` EQUALS "imprisonment for a term not exceeding 120 months"
`s16 threat to kill someone else` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `s16 threat to kill someone else`'s `the facts make out the offence`
`s16 lawful excuse` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s16 lawful excuse`'s `the facts make out the offence`)
#ASSERT `s16 lawful excuse`'s `what the Act calls it` EQUALS "Not made out on the facts you gave."
#ASSERT `s16 lawful excuse`'s `how the Act says it is tried` EQUALS "Not applicable."
#ASSERT `s16 lawful excuse`'s `the most the Act states can follow` EQUALS "Nothing follows from this section on the facts you gave."
`s16 no wish that they fear it` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s16 no wish that they fear it`'s `the facts make out the offence`)
`s16 a threat, but not to kill` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s16 a threat, but not to kill`'s `the facts make out the offence`)
`s16 no threat at all` MEANS `Could a threat I made amount to the offence of threatening to kill` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s16 no threat at all`'s `the facts make out the offence`)

§§ `Endangering life and causing bodily harm`

-- inputs, in order: 1 my act was unlawful | 2 my act was malicious | 3 I stopped or impeded someone saving their own life from a ship in distress | 4 I stopped or impeded someone saving the life of such a person
